Grammar of Graphics: Code Template
Let’s look at how these five main components show up in ggplot2 code:
# code template

ggplot(data = &lt;DATA&gt;) +
  &lt;GEOM&gt;(
    mapping = aes(&lt;MAPPING&gt;),
    stat = &lt;STAT&gt;,
    position = &lt;POSITION&gt;
  )
At the plot level, you can also add more components, like:
+ &lt;COORDINATE SYSTEM&gt;
+ &lt;FACET&gt;

Common Pipe Errors: Example 1
What’s wrong with this code?


Invalid code

delays &lt;- flights |&gt; 
  by_dest &lt;- group_by(dest) |&gt; 
  delay &lt;- summarize(
    count = n(),
    delay = mean(arr_delay, na.rm = TRUE)) |&gt; 
  d &lt;- filter(count &gt; 20)


Correct code

delays &lt;- flights |&gt;
  group_by(dest) |&gt;
  summarize(
    count = n(),
    delay = mean(arr_delay, na.rm = TRUE)) |&gt;
  filter(count &gt; 20)









Important


Don’t assign anything inside a pipe: use &lt;- only at the start, if you want to save the final result.

Common Pipe Errors: Example 3
What’s wrong with this code?


Invalid code

delays &lt;- flights |&gt; 
  group_by(flights, dest) |&gt; 
  summarize(flights,
    count = n(),
    delay = mean(arr_delay, na.rm = TRUE)) |&gt; 
  filter(count &gt; 20)

Common Pipe Errors: Example 3
What’s wrong with this code?


Invalid code

delays &lt;- flights |&gt; 
  group_by(flights, dest) |&gt; 
  summarize(flights,
    count = n(),
    delay = mean(arr_delay, na.rm = TRUE)) |&gt; 
  filter(count &gt; 20)


Correct code

delays &lt;- flights |&gt;
  group_by(dest) |&gt;
  summarize(
    count = n(),
    delay = mean(arr_delay, na.rm = TRUE)) |&gt;
  filter(count &gt; 20)









Important


When using pipes, only reference the data frame at the start of the chain. Do not repeat it in every function as |&gt; automatically passes the data along.

group_by()
# group by alone? not that useful
penguins |&gt; 
  group_by(island, sex)

# group by and calculate mean body mass
penguins |&gt; 
  group_by(island, sex) |&gt;
  summarize(body_mass_mean = mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE))

# group by and count
penguins |&gt; 
  group_by(island, sex) |&gt; 
  count()

count() as shortcut

# total penguins by sex without count
penguins |&gt; 
  group_by(sex) |&gt; 
  summarize(total = n())

# total penguins by sex with count
penguins |&gt;
  count(sex)

Using count() as shortcut

# total penguins by sex without count
penguins |&gt; 
  group_by(sex) |&gt; 
  summarize(total = n())

# total penguins by sex with count
penguins |&gt;
  count(sex)

select() plus filter()
penguins |&gt; 
  select(species, sex, year, island) |&gt; 
  filter(island == "Torgersen")

Exampels of arrange()
penguins |&gt; arrange(desc(bill_length_mm))

penguins |&gt;
  select(species, contains("bill")) |&gt;
  filter(!species == "Adelie") |&gt;
  arrange(desc(bill_length_mm)) 

penguins |&gt;
  select(species, contains("bill")) |&gt;
  filter(!species == "Adelie") |&gt; 
  arrange(desc(species), bill_length_mm)

Combining Verbs with the Pipe
Often we want to chain verbs together with the pipe |&gt;. This combination comes up frequently:
Key combo: filter() + group_by() + summarize()
penguins |&gt; 
  filter(species != "Gentoo") |&gt; 
  group_by(island) |&gt; 
  summarize(body_mass_mean = mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE))
You can also narrow down columns first with select(), then repeat the same combo
penguins |&gt; 
  select(species, island, body_mass_g) |&gt; 
  filter(species != "Gentoo") |&gt; 
  group_by(island) |&gt; 
  summarize(body_mass_mean = mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE))

Exampels of pull()
This won’t work
avg_mass &lt;- penguins |&gt;
  summarise(avg = mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE))

penguins |&gt; filter(body_mass_g &gt; avg_mass)
With pull it will work
avg_mass &lt;- penguins |&gt;
  summarise(avg = mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE)) |&gt;
  pull(avg)

penguins |&gt; filter(body_mass_g &gt; avg_mass)

Why does pull() make the difference?
Let’s ask R what avg_mass actually is in each case using class():
Without pull()
avg_mass &lt;- penguins |&gt;
  summarise(avg = mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE))

class(avg_mass)
#&gt; [1] "tbl_df"     "tbl"        "data.frame"
With pull()
avg_mass &lt;- penguins |&gt;
  summarise(avg = mean(body_mass_g, na.rm = TRUE)) |&gt;
  pull(avg)

class(avg_mass)
#&gt; [1] "numeric"
The verb summarize() always returns a tibble (a data frame), even when it holds a single value. So without pull(), the comparison becomes filter(body_mass_g &gt; &lt;1 x 1 tibble&gt;), which does not work in R. Why?

Comparison operators like &gt; inside filter() need vectors. In R, a single number is still a vector. The comparison body_mass_g &gt; avg_mass returns a logical vector (TRUE/FALSE for each row), which filter() uses to keep rows that are TRUE
pull() takes the column out of the tibble and gives you a plain vector, so the comparison works

Method 3: Reorder the bars using tidyverse fct_infreq()
fct_infreq() sorts bars automatically by how common each category is (most to least frequent)
scorecard |&gt;
  mutate(type = fct_infreq(type)) |&gt;
ggplot(aes(x = type)) +
  geom_bar()

